281:The motor is the Jinlong W0525AB001D (brushless coin, 5.0 mm diameter x 2.5 mm, 3.0 V, below 80 mA running, below 175 mA at each start, starts below 2.5 V [Jinlong spec S-525113 rev 0, architecture sections 2 and 17.16]) in the joystick-side shoulder of the band at -44 degrees (architecture 17.15), joined by two lead wires to flex tail A (J_BAND_A pad 7 MOT+ on VMOT, pad 6 MOT- on GND, netlist.csv).
282:- **Drive** (architecture 17.22; motor-drive.md section 2.1; netlist.csv): the motor runs from its own 3.0 V regulator U11 (Texas Instruments TLV75530PDQNR), whose input is the cell (net VBAT) and whose output is the net VMOT that feeds the motor. The pin MOTOR drives U11's EN pin (pin 3) through R61 100 ohm on the net MOT_EN, with R62 100 kilo-ohm from MOT_EN to ground, so the motor stays off while the chip starts and in `SHIP` and `OFF`. MOTOR high = motor on at 3.0 V (2.964 to 3.036 V for a cell at 3.15 V or more, motor-drive.md section 2.3); MOTOR low = U11 off. There is no PWM: the motor's maker rates it only for 2.7 to 3.3 V DC, and a pulse train would restart its own drive chip at each on time (an estimate of motor-drive.md section 3.2).
284:- **Outside the power chip's battery limit**: the motor's current flows from the cell through U11 and never through the nPM1304's battery path, so the chip's battery current limit IBATLIM (section 8.7) does not see it (architecture 17.22).
292:| M3 | low battery (B15, B5), storage S30 or SFULL, an action refused | 80 on, 120 off, 80 on, 120 off, 80 on | 480 |
299:- A pulse draws below 80 mA while the motor runs and up to 175 mA at its start, from the cell through U11, outside the power chip's 100 mA battery limit (motor-drive.md). On the smallest cell, 18.2 mAh at US 7, that is 4.4 C running and 9.6 C at the start; on the largest, 32.9 mAh at US 13, 2.4 C and 5.3 C [CALC: 80 / 18.2, 175 / 18.2, 80 / 32.9, 175 / 32.9; architecture 17.16, 17.17]; whether the cell and its protection circuit allow it is hardware issue H7.
300:- **Motor charge for the fuel gauge** (motor-drive.md section 2.8 change F3): the power chip measures the battery current inside its own path [NPM p99] and does not see the motor. The firmware adds, for every ON pulse of a pattern, the charge t_on x 80 mA + 10 ms x 175 mA (the second term is the start allowance, an **[ESTIMATE]** until bench step B-M3 of motor-drive.md section 2.9 measures the start; each pulse is counted as a start **[CHOICE]**: an over-count if the motor still turns after a gap of 120 ms or more, which no file states). Per pattern [CALC, `design/firmware-f5-notes.md`]: M1 8.15 mA.s, M2 16.30, M3 24.45, MH 4.95, MT 3.75, MW 6.55, MF 51.50 mA.s. How the sum enters the gauge is section 8.3.
308:- Cost: light current (3.0 V - 1.767 V) / (820 ohm + 0.7 ohm) = 1.50 mA from VDD_3V0 [CALC; 1.767 V at 1.5 mA from the maker's curve and R41 820 ohm, architecture 17.21; 0.7 ohm is Q2, sch-notes section 12.1 item 20] = 1.50 x 3.0 / (3.8 x 0.9) = 1.32 mA from the cell [CALC], only while recording. The recording current of 4.0 mA in architecture 17.15 already counts 1.5 mA for the light, so the budget keeps it (section 8.5). F4 wrote (3.0 V - 2.0 V) / 680 ohm = 1.5 mA; with the light's real 1.767 V, 680 ohm gives 1.81 mA (architecture 17.21), which is why R41 became 820 ohm.
